<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Serbia (early medieval)</h1><p class="st-meta">780 CE – 969 CE (approx.) · capital Ras</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first Serbian state, ruled by the Vlastimirović dynasty in the inland Balkans and converted to Christianity in the 870s.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> settled in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> in the 7th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a> names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viseslav-of-serbia">Višeslav</a> as an early prince, usually dated to around 780, but the state becomes clear under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlastimir">Vlastimir</a>, who united Serbian tribes in the 830s and repelled an invasion by the Bulgarian khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/presian-i">Presian</a> around 839–842.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The ruling prince (knez) shared power with relatives who held districts, and dynastic quarrels were frequent. The principality lay between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a>, often acting as a client of one against the other. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutimir-of-serbia">Mutimir</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in the 870s, through Byzantine missions.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simeon-i-of-bulgaria">Simeon I of Bulgaria</a> conquered <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/serbia">Serbia</a> in 924. Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caslav-klonimirovic">Časlav</a> restored it with Byzantine help from the late 920s, but after his death in the mid-10th century the state fragmented and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Ras</a> around 970. Later Serbian states emerged in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duklja">Duklja</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grand-principality-of-serbia">Raška</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-serbia-early-medieval.jpg" alt="Principality of Serbia (early medieval)" width="320" height="210"><figcaption>Image: Hel-hama · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Europe_814.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>780 CE – 969 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ras</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Vlastimir, Mutimir, Časlav</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Slavic paganism; Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Serbian (Slavonic)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>, <a href="/countries/montenegro">Montenegro</a>, <a href="/countries/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Vlastimirović Serbia, Serbian Principality</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=874&amp;focus=principality-of-serbia-early-medieval" aria-label="Principality of Serbia (early medieval) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5172 647 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Serbia (early medieval) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5492 903L5444 916M5492 903L5709 970M5492 903L5653 895"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5444 916h0M5709 970h0M5653 895h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5492 903h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5492 903h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=874&amp;focus=principality-of-serbia-early-medieval">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

